This distribution contains no third-party source code.

It does bundle device parameter files, under `devices/` and
`heatsweep/devices/`, whose numeric values are transcribed from, or
estimated from, the third-party sources below. Those values are factual
measurements and published specifications rather than third-party
expression, and each file records its own provenance and test conditions
in the accompanying README and TOML. The sources are listed here for
attribution.

Oak Ridge National Laboratory, operated by UT-Battelle, LLC for the
U.S. Department of Energy:

  ORNL/TM-2004/247   Evaluation of 2004 Toyota Prius Hybrid Electric
                     Drive System (Interim). Drivetrain loss
                     decomposition, back-EMF vs speed.
  ORNL/TM-2005/33    Report on Toyota Prius Motor Thermal Management
  (FY2005)           (900 rpm line-fed, 35–105 °C coolant).
  ORNL/TM-2006/423   Evaluation of the 2004 Toyota Prius hybrid electric
                     drive system. Inverter packaging, die photography,
                     thermal stack, full-envelope dyno data.
  ORNL/TM-2010/253   Die measurements, SEM cross-sections, solder EDS
                     analysis, 2004 vs 2010 thermal comparison,
                     continuous duty.
  ORNL/TM-2013/498   Benchmarking State-of-the-Art Technologies.
                     Cross-vehicle design comparison.
  ORNL/SPR-2014/532  Electrical and Electronic Technical Team Roadmap.
                     HEV benchmarking context.
  DOE Annual Merit Review APE006 (2013), T. Burress and Z. Liang —
                     2012 Nissan LEAF inverter and motor data.

Manufacturer datasheets, used as technology and current-rating analogues
where no measured Prius device data exists:

  International Rectifier IRG4PC50U (600 V / 55 A NPT IGBT)
  Mitsubishi CM100DU-12F (600 V / 100 A F-series IGBT module)

Parameter values derived from these sources are estimates unless the
device file marks them measured. They are not endorsed by, and imply no
affiliation with, ORNL, UT-Battelle, the U.S. Department of Energy,
Toyota, Nissan, International Rectifier, or Mitsubishi Electric.
